bw-port: the 3D renderer row says how Fast draws, or why it cannot. Renderer3D=fast now draws on GL too (port/gpu
g10_fast_gl.c: framebuffer fetch on GLES and Mesa, fragment interlock or INTEL ordering on desktop GL 4.5; docs/gpu-hwraster.md
"GL"), so the bridge reports what the core found at the first frame that wanted it: SIM_Renderer3DFast(ok, how) (port/bridge
gpu_bridge.c), read back by SIM_Renderer3DFastState(&how) (the iOS sheet and the Android screen) and SIM_UISettingGet("fast3d")
(1 drawn, 0 cannot, -1 not tried). The Video window's row: "Not on this GPU (why): the exact renderer draws", or on GL the way
it draws ("OpenGL ES: framebuffer fetch"); the row's help no longer says Metal only.
--- a/libraries/sim/src/sim_main.cpp
+++ b/libraries/sim/src/sim_main.cpp
@@ -551,6 +551,19 @@
   return s_SIM_config.antialias == 3 ? 3 : s_SIM_config.antialias >= 2 ? 2 : 1;
 }
 extern "C" void SIM_RendererAAUsed(int k) { sAAUsed = k; }
+// bw-port (libntr-ios-fastgl.patch): Renderer3D=fast on the GPU renderer's core, as the bridge found it at the first frame that
+// wanted it: 1 it draws fast, 0 it cannot (the exact renderer draws), -1 not tried yet; how, or why not, in the core's words
+// ("Metal", "OpenGL ES: framebuffer fetch", "this GLES has no EXT_shader_framebuffer_fetch", ...: port/gpu gpu_fast_how)
+static int sFast3D = -1;
+static char sFast3DHow[200];
+extern "C" void SIM_Renderer3DFast(int ok, const char *how) {
+  snprintf(sFast3DHow, sizeof sFast3DHow, "%s", how != NULL ? how : "");
+  sFast3D = ok;
+}
+extern "C" int SIM_Renderer3DFastState(const char **how) {
+  if (how != NULL) *how = sFast3DHow;
+  return sFast3D;
+}
